#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5575" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069030/assets/226d83de1fff110426877af083e1aee5eac7f7de1244bb59f7f801aeea8ec6a5.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069030/assets/c051729499bde9389ad7e38ab91d3609c8153cbed59233f4b3e3e7ac9e9c2534.png)\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}26 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5576" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069030/assets/df90590ac6801e6f2efaada175e5d8460a45cb06375064eb53f80eddbbd3991c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A\\left(A^T\\!A\\right)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ In our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069030/assets/a1592d17b242a66e66948692dfaf7892f9df6d415ae96fb59154455ffe1a4ef9.png)\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{32}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a498902-85c6-5f89-883e-a85e9e36af5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360079} {:answer/id #uuid "37291497-80d8-55fd-a719-2f8271551118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360080} {:answer/id #uuid "a4152323-3f5a-5239-b473-9012441c929e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360081} {:answer/id #uuid "6f094c64-d945-5962-9bb0-fc67c85dac21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360082} {:answer/id #uuid "50f4c4fa-4cba-5b8a-b3c3-c3801cdf2dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f094c64-d945-5962-9bb0-fc67c85dac21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1643379-feea-50ae-b265-4e642bed09f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd7403df-a496-59c6-8226-c07a3e6dd8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158123" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6992c1b7-d169-52f6-9bba-a7182a85e32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360086} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bbbd68-7fc6-528a-9327-3e6c2753e4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360087} {:answer/id #uuid "1831f40d-a022-50db-a924-ad36e8473bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360088} {:answer/id #uuid "6da6cf4f-435c-5ede-8a38-3e68a1d621fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360089} {:answer/id #uuid "7332c187-dbe0-52d0-a541-44f26ad77d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bbbd68-7fc6-528a-9327-3e6c2753e4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360087} :answer-field/id #uuid "6adf1085-d2a9-58f1-9d58-adcf99e522a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be4dd768-6510-560f-b2de-05e4cf6c4956" :question/math-academy-id "q-158359" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceced9c-105a-5ab3-aff0-250feacaa3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360093} {:answer/id #uuid "8539903a-77bf-5d56-945a-94b1edfc432a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360094} {:answer/id #uuid "146434a3-246e-5543-8016-746775576406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360095} {:answer/id #uuid "8306223f-1fc1-58cb-bcc7-0c09fba9d1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360096} {:answer/id #uuid "38965953-77f8-54c4-af90-26afc2efc9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38965953-77f8-54c4-af90-26afc2efc9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360097} :answer-field/id #uuid "357b85b1-b846-5c54-8e51-ea316dd94fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51493b5e-5437-5dda-a56f-ba4f747b7597" :question/math-academy-id "q-158362" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91018703-02af-5427-b8ea-7b7e51a9811d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360100} {:answer/id #uuid "78cfae3e-03de-5cc6-8cc2-405f07fd3838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360101} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2a00e8-e673-57cd-9470-a797da27d539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360102} {:answer/id #uuid "fd91d4e9-0316-51f9-8e1a-3ec508fa5fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360103} {:answer/id #uuid "492f12a1-116e-5f76-8362-c28d70f34293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91018703-02af-5427-b8ea-7b7e51a9811d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360100} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3a8569-a777-56bc-9a83-9acf5010552e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6102ade5-8e07-551c-bd72-f8a53d7fcf98" :question/math-academy-id "q-39871" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where   \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{32}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdd6106-99d9-5541-a7ae-46cafa6cfdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131769} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78dd9f-8345-5de7-adc6-54711225fd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131770} {:answer/id #uuid "1110c3eb-0f6d-549e-acb7-1ea49b28760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131771} {:answer/id #uuid "163e132e-4669-594a-9930-7dc12dcaff6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131772} {:answer/id #uuid "20b8fbcc-ffb8-5bb1-ba18-6a8700e54b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1110c3eb-0f6d-549e-acb7-1ea49b28760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131771} :answer-field/id #uuid "b70ed4bf-2cc2-5089-aa64-7bbf0cfc1b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22c16d4d-ef3b-582d-a66e-91e72ba35f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-40570" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e93c88-689c-5bac-a8f2-f4d6d1003f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360107} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb4ded0-6216-5778-a5a8-201b230ffcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360108} {:answer/id #uuid "fa940451-077d-5e04-be54-642acdbec1c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360109} {:answer/id #uuid "26942890-7698-5315-97c6-3a5fbe55b32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360110} {:answer/id #uuid "6a37da01-6008-5a87-a961-b45f6226fe15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a37da01-6008-5a87-a961-b45f6226fe15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360111} :answer-field/id #uuid "441ab859-f2a4-5670-8eea-6df23a720592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82da0a25-7594-5b06-8db8-199ee0a472d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-40571" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}27 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dbde84-b7fd-5a49-b95d-2de167e27fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360114} {:answer/id #uuid "3829567d-7f52-5dfb-a6e1-aa1221912788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360115} {:answer/id #uuid "e3407b3a-81f7-514a-b9b6-612575725a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360116} {:answer/id #uuid "918b0a4d-0f1f-5e11-93cb-46a9fc73cd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360117} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2f2d1b-04c1-5f4e-822d-cb4d0b2ca568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae2f2d1b-04c1-5f4e-822d-cb4d0b2ca568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360118} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb969f8-d541-50e1-9c02-ae2805db722b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd4e9ae3-e828-50c3-898a-1dacef23b6a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40573" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979cda63-7a7c-583f-a946-666d941be1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360121} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e58591-bdd1-5a83-bd56-d0bd00cb458b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360122} {:answer/id #uuid "889971ae-0603-517d-b871-01bf52522d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360123} {:answer/id #uuid "0987ed84-5fcb-5d02-88ce-86e10359ae70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360124} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d8e69d-a74d-5529-8da2-78aecf8f2490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1d8e69d-a74d-5529-8da2-78aecf8f2490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360125} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0a5268-bdd5-5871-9b8b-a0b20b27449d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c071401-3e46-54b6-b9cb-b8280a1a58b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40630" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ff52d8-650f-5f47-879d-8e6ec2bb59b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360128} {:answer/id #uuid "60d60b65-ce50-55ab-86ad-9c4c7f324e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360129} {:answer/id #uuid "db3b4907-20ac-55df-add5-5c7a8ce2ad23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360130} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4cc7ed-705b-53e5-bd7b-5f95b2262c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360131} {:answer/id #uuid "32591392-3089-51d2-a8e4-732c18c14988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d60b65-ce50-55ab-86ad-9c4c7f324e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360129} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d08415-a1e2-549f-8ae4-748444ba11f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f07d051f-511e-591e-9291-2d697e2bdb0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83581" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6470de8-62c6-5897-a66b-9f69232ae5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360135} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b34fb-0b25-584e-a03c-d8adc6a7e894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360136} {:answer/id #uuid "237671ac-0c39-5d5c-82e7-9a446be06b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360137} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c5f301-90ef-52aa-bcbe-a93fa9d0ee89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360138} {:answer/id #uuid "b08abede-9445-51b3-8f9c-c04deb7d9264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b34fb-0b25-584e-a03c-d8adc6a7e894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360136} :answer-field/id #uuid "867cb97a-f9c8-55a8-8e79-013c44385e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e17a352-7563-56a2-b8d0-276efd4813f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86760" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}14 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
